BRW-004 slice (a) egress-constraint probe #1 (2026-09-04): can the sandbox provider constrain
a sandbox's OUTBOUND egress at all? BRW-004-terrain §12 lists this among the things it could
NOT establish, and design §D3 option (b) plus §7 Q2 both rest on the answer. Source cannot
settle it: AoA's only egress-shaped provider input, `SandboxProviderAcquireInput.egressAllowlist`,
is written into E2B `Sandbox.create` metadata as a comma-joined string, and its producer's own
comments call it "NOT a security boundary" — a claim this probe MEASURES rather than quotes.

Differential by construction: sandbox A declares `metadata.egressAllowlist`, sandbox B does
not, and both run the identical target set from inside the guest. The allowlist has an effect
only if the not-allowlisted target behaves differently in A than in B. A positive control (the
allowlisted host must succeed in A) and an apparatus control (an RFC-2606 `.invalid` host must
fail) both gate the verdict, so a run in which everything fails is reported INCONCLUSIVE rather
than read as enforcement.

probe #2 (2026-09-04): run 33855470353 was correctly reported INCONCLUSIVE — the apparatus
control produced NO RESULT LINE in both arms while the three earlier targets returned
normally. That is the control doing exactly its job: without it, "not_allowlisted REACHED in
both arms" would have read as a finished measurement while the probe was silently dropping a
row. Chaining all four targets into one `sh -c` meant whatever swallowed the fourth erased it
with no trace. Fixed by running ONE command per target, each ending `exit 0` so a failing curl
cannot throw at the SDK layer (E7-F014: real E2B THROWS on non-zero exit rather than
returning), with a PROBE/END-delimited line so a truncated line is rejected rather than
half-read.

Carried forward from run #1 as a real observation, independent of the verdict: the cloud
instance-metadata endpoint at 169.254.169.254 ANSWERED from inside the sandbox with HTTP 401
in BOTH arms. It is reachable; only IMDSv2's token requirement stood between the guest and it.

probe #3 (2026-09-04): run 33856410800 printed every target's RAW channel and found it in one
line. The apparatus control's record came back as TWO lines from the Linux guest —
"curl: (6) Could not resolve host: ..." then "000" — because curl writes -w output to stdout
and the error to stderr and 2>&1 interleaves them. On Windows the same command emits them on
ONE line ("000curl: (6) ..."), which is exactly why the local dry-run passed and the real
sandbox did not. A dry-run on a different platform is not a control for the real one. Fixed in
BOTH producer and consumer: the shell flattens the diagnostic to one line (reading $? from the
assignment, never through a pipe, or $? would be tr's), and the parser matches across newlines
so the record is assembled rather than silently lost if anything reintroduces one.

probe #4 (2026-09-04) — THE MEASUREMENT, run 33857218680, success, all four rows parsed and both
controls satisfied:

  allowlisted (positive control)  A: REACHED 200   B: REACHED 200
  not_allowlisted (the question)  A: REACHED 200   B: REACHED 200
  link_local_metadata             A: REACHED 401   B: REACHED 401
  unresolvable (apparatus ctrl)   A: FAILED curl 6 B: FAILED curl 6

VERDICT — the metadata allowlist is INERT. A host that is NOT on the declared allowlist was reached
from inside the sandbox that declared it, exactly as from the control sandbox that declared none.
The provider does not constrain outbound egress through the seam AoA production uses, so BRW-004
design §D3 option (b) is unavailable and the in-sandbox enforcement point (c) is the ONLY layer
rather than defence in depth.

OBSERVATION, and it is the more serious half: the cloud instance-metadata endpoint answered HTTP 401
from inside the guest in both arms — a live IMDSv2 token challenge, not a dropped packet. Filed as
E8-F003 (HIGH, unowned).
